Reason:
The original condition only checked if a level was not loading (`!packetHandler.isLoadingLevel`), which did not account for the scenario where the game was in singleplayer mode and the menu needed to be displayed. This oversight caused the menu to not show up when the escape key was pressed in singleplayer.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```java
if (Keyboard.getEventKey() == Keyboard.KEY_ESCAPE) {
    if (!packetHandler.isLoadingLevel || !isOnline()) {
        pause();
    }
}
```
Reason:
The fix modifies the condition to check if the game is not loading a level or if the game is not in online mode (`!isOnline()`). This ensures that the pause function is called correctly in singleplayer mode, allowing the menu to be displayed when the escape key is pressed.

